=1$?","options":["$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{(x-1)^n}{n!}$","$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{(x-1)^n}{n}$","$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{x^n}{n}$","$\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty\\frac{(x-1)^n}{n}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"23b93d24dc88530d9f08ae92","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_015_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which hypothesis on $f$ guarantees that the Taylor series of $f$ at $a$ converges to $f(x)$ for every $x$ in some interval around $a$?","options":["$f$ is real analytic at $a$.","$f$ has derivatives of every order on an interval around $a$.","$f$ is differentiable on an interval around $a$.","$f$ is continuous and bounded on an interval around $a$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"402c3356437daa20040e5ccc","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_fill220261112_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"The function $\\ln(1+x)$ is defined for all $x>-1$, and its Taylor series at $0$ is $\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}x^n$, with radius of convergence $1$. For which $x$ does this series converge?","options":["For all $x>-1$, where $\\ln(1+x)$ is defined","For $-1<x<1$ only","For $-1\\le x\\le1$","For $-1<x\\le1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"17d0808c527a912a1d667944","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_series_d714ffe8_fill220261112_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"The Maclaurin series of $\\mathrm{e}^{x^2}$ is obtained by substituting $x^2$ into the exponential series: $\\mathrm{e}^{x^2}=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{x^{2n}}{n!}$ for every real $x$. Let $f(x)=\\mathrm{e}^{x^2}$. What is $f^{(6)}(0)$?","options":["$\\frac{1}{6}$","$120$","$1$","$720$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"243bac768f09621778b340d8"